oup-type="author"><name><surname>McCord</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Chowdhury</surname><given-names>Q</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A cost effective small hospital in Bangladesh: what it can mean for emergency obstetric care</article-title><source>Int J Gynaecol Obstet</source><year>2003</year><volume>81</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>83</fpage><lpage>92</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0020-7292(03)00072-9</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">McCord C, Chowdhury Q (2003) A cost effective small hospital in Bangladesh: what it can mean for emergency obstetric care. Int J Gynaecol Obstet 81(1):83&#x02013;92 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12676406</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR16"><label>16.</label><citation citation-type="other">Anon. (2007) Bellagio report: conference on increasing access to surgical services in resource-constrained settings in sub-Saharan Africa, summary report, June 4&#x02013;8, 2007, Bellagio, Italy. Available at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.dcp2.org/file/137/Bellagio%20Report%20-%20Increasing%20Access%20to%20Surgical%20Services.pdf">http://www.dcp2.org/file/137/Bellagio%20Report%20-%20Increasing%20Access%20to%20Surgical%20Services.pdf</ext-link> (accessed 22 December 2007)</citation></ref><ref id="CR17"><label>17.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Samb</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Celletti</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Holloway</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Rapid expansion of the health workforce in response to the HIV epidemic</article-title><source>N Engl J Med</source><year>2007</year><volume>357</volume><issue>24</issue><fpage>2510</fpage><lpage>2514</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1056/NEJMsb071889</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Samb B, Celletti F, Holloway J et&#x000a0;al (2007) Rapid expansion of the health workforce in response to the HIV epidemic. N Engl J Med 357(24):2510&#x02013;2514 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18077816</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR18"><label>18.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Walton</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><name><surname>Farmer</surname><given-names>PE</given-names></name><name><surname>Dillingham</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Guerrant</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name><name><surname>Walker</surname><given-names>DH</given-names></name><name><surname>Weller</surname><given-names>PF</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Social and cultural factors in tropical medicine: reframing our understanding of disease</article-title><source>Tropical infectious diseases: principles, pathogens, and practice</source><year>2005</year><edition>2</edition><publisher-loc>New York</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Elsevier</publisher-name><fpage>26</fpage><lpage>35</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Walton DA, Farmer PE, Dillingham R (2005) Social and cultural factors in tropical medicine: reframing our understanding of disease. In: Guerrant RL, Walker DH, Weller PF (eds) Tropical infectious diseases: principles, pathogens, and practice, 2nd ed. Elsevier, New York pp 26&#x02013;35 </citation></ref><ref id="CR19"><label>19.</label><citation citation-type="other">Physicians for Human Rights (2004) An action plan to prevent brain drain: building equitable health systems in Africa. Boston: Physicians for Human Rights. Available at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.physiciansforhumanrights.org/library/documents/reports/report-2004-july.pdf">http://www.physiciansforhumanrights.org/library/documents/reports/report-2004-july.pdf</ext-link> (accessed 10 January 2008)</citation></ref><ref id="CR20"><label>20.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Evans</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Anand</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Human resources for health: overcoming the crisis</article-title><source>Lancet</source><year>2004</year><volume>364</volume><issue>9449</issue><fpage>1984</fpage><lpage>1990</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0140-6736(04)17482-5</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Chen L, Evans T, Anand S et&#x000a0;al (2004) Human resources for health: overcoming the crisis. Lancet 364(9449):1984&#x02013;1990 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15567015</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR21"><label>21.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ncayiyana</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Doctors and nurses with HIV and AIDS in sub-Saharan Africa</article-title><source>BMJ</source><year>2004</year><volume>329</volume><issue>7466</issue><fpage>584</fpage><lpage>585</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1136/bmj.329.7466.584</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Ncayiyana D (2004) Doctors and nurses with HIV and AIDS in sub-Saharan Africa. BMJ 329(7466):584&#x02013;585 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15361421</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR22"><label>22.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Raviola</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Machoki</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Mwaikambo</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>HIV, disease plague, demoralization and &#x0201c;burnout&#x0201d;: resident experience of the medical profession in Nairobi, Kenya</article-title><source>Cult Med Psychiatry</source><year>2002</year><volume>26</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>55</fpage><lpage>86</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1023/A:1015289132151</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Raviola G, Machoki M, Mwaikambo E et&#x000a0;al (2002) HIV, disease plague, demoralization and &#x0201c;burnout&#x0201d;: resident experience of the medical profession in Nairobi, Kenya. Cult Med Psychiatry 26(1):55&#x02013;86 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12088098</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR23"><label>23.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Furin</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Farmer</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Wolf</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>A novel training model to address health problems in poor and underserved populations</article-title><source>J Health Care Poor Underserved</source><year>2006</year><volume>17</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>17</fpage><lpage>24</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1353/hpu.2006.0023</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Furin J, Farmer P, Wolf M et&#x000a0;al (2006) A novel training model to address health problems in poor and underserved populations. J Health Care Poor Underserved 17(1):17&#x02013;24 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16520503</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR24"><label>24.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Farmer</surname><given-names>PE</given-names></name><name><surname>Furin</surname><given-names>JJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Katz</surname><given-names>JT</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Global health equity</article-title><source>Lancet</source><year>2004</year><volume>363</volume><issue>9423</issue><fpage>1832</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0140-6736(04)16325-3</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Farmer PE, Furin JJ, Katz JT (2004) Global health equity. Lancet 363(9423):1832 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15172796</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR25"><label>25.</label><citation citation-type="other">World Health Organization (2005) World health report 2005: making every mother and child count. Available at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.who.int/whr/2005/whr2005_en.pdf">http://www.who.int/whr/2005/whr2005_en.pdf</ext-link> (accessed 4 January 2008)</citation></ref><ref id="CR26"><label>26.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Spiegel</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><name><surname>Gosselin</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Surgical services in low-income and middle-income countries</article-title><source>Lancet</source><year>2007</year><volume>370</volume><issue>9592</issue><fpage>1013</fpage><lpage>1015</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0140-6736(07)61457-3</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Spiegel DA, Gosselin RA (2007) Surgical services in low-income and middle-income countries. Lancet 370(9592):1013&#x02013;1015 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17889228</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR27"><label>27.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Duda</surname><given-names>RB</given-names></name><name><surname>Hill</surname><given-names>AG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Surgery in developing countries: should surgery have a role in population-based health care?</article-title><source>Bull Am Coll Surg</source><year>2006</year><volume>92</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>12</fpage><lpage>18</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Duda RB, Hill AG (2006) Surgery in developing countries: should surgery have a role in population-based health care? Bull Am Coll Surg 92(6):12&#x02013;18, 35 </citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>